# The two figures of every graph the filings make that has an edge, across the whole corpus. One
# row per graph: nodes, edges, components, `cycle_space_dim` and `rank_of_incidence`. The claimant
# graph has no edges, because delegation is filed nowhere, so it has no row.
#
# What the graphs differ on
#   `cycle_space_dim` counts a graph's independent loops; `rank_of_incidence` is its nodes less its
#   components. A graph with loops has no evaluation order (rank/evaluation_order.sqlc gives its
#   layers NULL), and it still has a `rank_of_incidence`. The two are different figures, and taking
#   one for the other gives a wrong claim: that a graph with loops has no `rank_of_incidence` at
#   all. It has one.
#
# Loops without direction, and the implication runs one way
#   `cycle_space_dim` counts loops with the direction of the edges ignored, so a zero says the graph
#   has no loops at all. Whether anything descends forever is the question with direction, and
#   every graph with no directed loop answers it no. A graph with no loops descends forever nowhere;
#   the reverse does not hold. A diamond is four edges over four nodes in one component, so it has
#   one loop, and nothing in it descends forever. So a zero here gives you an evaluation order, and
#   an evaluation order gives you back nothing. The layer graph has both at once, and that is
#   `checks/jagged_layer` holding it there, not something that must be so: a diamond in it is a
#   layer composed twice.
#
# Where a rule round a loop lives
#   A rule written as a walk round a loop is a condition on the loops. For the unit graph, the
#   factors filed round a loop must come back to where they started: going round, the conversions
#   multiply to one, which is what lets every unit have one size consistent with the factors filed
#   between them. `checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close` asks the form for ranges: with each
#   factor a range, that 1 lie inside the product round the loop, since closing exactly at the most
#   likely value is out of reach, as that rule's header shows.
#
# And a balance at a node is where this model's other rule lives
#   This model has one rule of each kind. A product round a loop is the loop condition, the
#   paragraph above, and what `checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close` asks of the unit graph. A
#   balance at a node is the other: what a composed layer files equals the sum over its converted
#   parts less what was eliminated. That is the fusion rule, and `checks/jagged_layer` accuses on
#   that side. So a filing can satisfy one and break the other, and the two graphs need two rules
#   rather than one.
#
# No loop, nothing goes round
#   Where `cycle_space_dim` is zero, every amount on the graph's edges is a difference between its
#   nodes, and nothing goes round. That is supply counted once, and it is why nothing here can grow
#   by going round: a composition has no direction to circulate in. Read the figures off this
#   relation rather than from a sentence.
#
# The numbers
#   Run this relation for the counts. The corpus moves and a sentence does not.
#
# Components ignore direction
#   Both figures are about the graph with the direction of its edges ignored. Reading them off the
#   directed edges gives a different and wrong component count.
#
# One scope of `rank/graph_measures.sqlc`
#   This relation is that one across the whole corpus, not a second copy of the walk.
#   `rank/decomposition.sqlc` reads the same relation, and its `filing IS NULL` rows are exactly
#   these rows, so a fix to the walk is made once.
#
# The pin is `filing IS NULL`, and the column is dropped
#   That is the shape that usually marks a slice passing for a whole. It is declared here and in the
#   `--` line because it is the whole graph rather than a slice of one: the per-filing cut is a
#   different question, and `rank/decomposition.sqlc` is the file that asks it.
-- rank/graph_measures.sqlc across the whole corpus: the two graphs, counted whole.
SELECT m.graph, m.n_nodes, m.m_edges, m.c_components, m.cycle_space_dim, m.rank_of_incidence
FROM (
    :compose(rank/graph_measures.sqlc)
) m
WHERE m.filing IS NULL
